    /// <c>pie(x, labels = names, col = colors, clockwise = TRUE)</c>.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>Wedge i spans the fraction values[i] / sum(values) of the circle. Wedges start at 12
    /// o'clock and run clockwise (R's default starts at 3 o'clock and runs counter-clockwise).
    /// Each wedge is a filled unit-radius polygon with 24 arc segments. Names, when given, are
    /// drawn at radius 1.15 beside the middle of each wedge's arc and also label the wedges, so the
    /// automatic legend lists them. Colors are recycled; empty uses an 8-color built-in palette
    /// starting blue, red, green. The axes and grid are hidden and both ranges are fixed at
    /// [-1.4, 1.4]. The pie is drawn in data units, so it is round only when the plotting area is
    /// square, e.g. <c>.size(598, 640)</c> with the default margins; the default 960x640 canvas draws an
    /// ellipse.</para>
    /// <para><b>Example</b></para>
    /// <code>
    /// auto plt = dm::Plot::pie({3.0, 2.0, 1.0}, {"rent", "food", "fun"}).size(598, 640);
    /// std::print("{}\n", plt.series().size());  // 6
    /// plt.save("budget.svg");
    /// </code>
    /// <para><b>References</b></para>
    /// <para>- R <c>pie()</c>: https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/graphics/html/pie.html</para>
    /// <para>See also: <c>polygon()</c>, <c>text()</c>.</para>
    /// <para>Uses the C++ default for <c>names = default</c>, <c>colors = default</c>.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="values">Wedge sizes; nonnegative, with a positive sum.</param>
    /// <returns>A new plot holding one Polygon series per wedge plus one Text series per name.</returns>
    /// <exception cref="NativeException">C++ <c>std::invalid_argument</c>: If <c>values</c> is empty, has a negative entry, or sums to 0, or <c>names</c> is nonempty with a different length.</exception>
    public static RPlot Pie(IEnumerable<double> values) => Rosetta.To<RPlot>(Rosetta.Call(1052, values));

