    /// <summary>
    /// One-sample Student t-test of a population mean, like R t.test().
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>Independent normal observations give t=(mean-mu)/SE with n-1 df. The CI estimates the
    /// population mean, not mean-mu. Inputs are copied only for paired differences; all routines
    /// leave inputs unchanged and use no shared mutable state. O(n) work; no null/nonfinite omission.</para>
    /// <para><b>Example</b></para>
    /// <code>
    /// const auto t=dm::stats::t_test_one_sample({1,2,3,4});
    /// std::print("{:.0f}\n",t.parameter1); // 3
    /// </code>
    /// <para><b>References</b></para>
    /// <para>- https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/t.test.html</para>
    /// <para>See also: <c>HypothesisTestResult</c>.</para>
    /// <para>Uses the C++ default for <c>mu = 0.0</c>, <c>alternative = Alternative.TwoSided</c>, <c>confLevel = 0.95</c>.</para>
    /// </remarks>
    /// <param name="x">At least two finite observations with positive representable sample variance.</param>
    /// <returns>statistic=t, parameter1=df, estimate1=sample mean, p_value and mean confidence bounds; remaining scalars retain defaults. One-sided intervals have one infinite endpoint.</returns>
    /// <exception cref="NativeException">C++ <c>std::invalid_argument</c>: Too few/nonfinite values, invalid mu/alternative/confidence or zero/nonfinite standard error.</exception>
    public static HypothesisTestResult TTestOneSample(IEnumerable<double> x) => Rosetta.To<HypothesisTestResult>(Rosetta.Call(201, x));

